e of the  other Gulf states and certainly to  Israel and Egypt we're talking with  Thomas L Friedman this is what the book  looks like as you can see it's from  Beirut to Jerusalem a book that you  completed on what date I completed it  though there was no one date since  between the editing and the copy editing  and the final proofing but it was  probably finished mark last March how  did you write it physically sitting at a  VDT physically i sat down at my IBM PC  and Jerusalem and when I started the  book and um just started typing do you  write in the morning or the evening I  would basically my scheduled B I'd get  up and read the morning paper have my  breakfast and morning coffee ride my  stationary bicycle and go into my office  close the door and try to write for  about three hours which is about as long  as I could concentrate in a really  productive way and then I would take a  break I'd go for a walk I'd eat lunch  and then I try to write for a couple  more hours in the afternoon and evening  did you rewrite everything that you  wrote once oh yeah everything you you  would read in the book here a  certainly has been written in some case  rewritten and rewritten and rewritten  and what what role does the editor play  in this and and do they really make a  big difference well I think again it  depends on the the writer what role the  editor plays some obviously I came at  the book with a lot of writing  experience I wasn't in any way in office  but at the same time my editor I think  played a very important role in helping  me with organization sometimes you don't  really see the best way to organize the  book as you're writing it and it's very  helpful to have someone say you know the  second half of chapter five would really  make a perfect you know first half of  chapter three and the bottom half of  chapter three should really be the  second half of chapter seven etc etc  it's only you know really sometimes an  outsider that can make those  recommendations for you did you have  days when you just did a block and you  couldn't write it all so definitely or  days that I just didn't have the psychic  energy to be creative I think every  author goes through that you just wake  up in the morning and you say I just  don't feel like writing now and then  other days you just can't stop did you  write from notes or write from the top  of your head both really I wrote from  articles that I had published already I  used to a small extent fresh interviews  and notes I'd taken to a large extent  and then just whatever came out of my  head that was rolling around back there  in the file cabinet what do you expect  to happen beyond the publication of this  book or anything else that comes out of  this book um there's going to be  obviously a paperback edition it was  sold last week it's going to be  published by Ankur Doubleday and there's  also going to be a Dutch Edition a  Hebrew Edition and a British edition of  the book and we expect more foreign  sales in the near future I hate to  mention such a thing on this program but  is there a television or a movie out of  this some people have thought about that  some portions of the book might be an in  make for an interesting show or movie or  whatever but I kind of doubt it but  there are people looking at it  that with that in mind how long have you  been back in Washington I've been here  now in the job right in the job since  January started the day Baker went in  for his confirmation hearings what do  you think of that  compared to this kind of thing well in  many ways I've gone from covering a  street to covering a hall that's really  how I would summarize the transition  from covering the Middle East covering  the State Department and gone from  covering a human drama that was  unfolding before my eyes really to  covering a policy that was invisible or  that is invisible and what is the policy  it's a collection of grunts and nods and  attitudes really you can't say I saw the  policy today it bled all over the  sidewalk I saw the policy today it wept  on my shoulder so as a journalist I  think you're you're much more  constrained in the kind of range of  emotions and and scenes which you're  going to be able to describe I mean  there's only so many ways to say an  administration official said at the same  time what I like about this story and  what I really truly enjoy about it is  that after ten years in the Middle East  you feel I've been to this play before I  know how it ends that the story always  seems to go in circles and the circle is  always downward what I like about my job  now so much is that it's open-ended who  knows where the story of us-soviet  relations is going to end who knows  where the story of Poland is gonna end  who knows where the story of China is  gonna end and what I like about the job  I have now is that it's new it's fresh  and it's open-ended correct me if I'm  wrong but two people that had your job  before have moved on to being involved  some very directly in policy Richard  Burt now is our ambassador the Startalk  system it was actually the national  security correspondent in the Washington  bureau not dumb the diplomat and he also  wanted to be an ambassador Germany right  and Leslie Gelb who who used to have a  similar kind of job personal security  course right it was a military fair the  editor of the page but he did a stint at  the State Department  in the department of political  military-first right is there a  political future for Thomas Frieden  right now I'm just happy as a clam as a  reporter covering the diplomatic beat  and traveling with the Secretary of  State who can who can predict what will  happen down the road but I guess I don't  want to pin you down on whether you're  now has the the ever get a sense that  being on the other side would be  interesting yes definitely I certainly  have had that sense before that at some  point I'd be fascinated to know what it  looks like from the inside I think any  journalist worth his salt wants to know  that too name's Charles Redmond and  Margaret Tong one reason I bring those  two names up and there are others  leaving some out John Hughes I think's  another one  and there are a lot that have pinched  hitted are people that are audience  again see all the time uh-huh you go to  those briefings at this they do or I do  indeed you get anything out of them  definitely definitely  what well in the immediate sense you get  the most immediate declaration of  American policy on any subject you want  to ask for and that's really what  they're important for they're important  for watching the policy involved now you  say wait a minute you know Mari you said  this on Tuesday you're saying this on  Wednesday there's been a shift or an  event happens overseas we want to know  how is our government reacting to the  new situation in Poland you go that the  noon briefing and you find out all right  when you watch and I'm a watcher you you  you watch the assistant secretary come  in and put her write papers down and  open up are there questions yes now as  she's sitting there fully briefed  waiting for you to ask otherwise you'll  never hear from her well or no we can we  can and do talk to Margaret both before  and after the briefing and throughout  the day if something breaks at five  o'clock or seven o'clock I'm on the  phone to her basically she comes in  there with what the various departments  in the State Department think will be  the questions that will ask  so say on a day like today where at the  time we're doing this record in Columbia  now is in the news and the South America  department will sit around early in the  morning and say what might reporters ask  about our policy in Columbia today they  try to anticipate the questions and then  they write guidance for Margaret to use  during the briefing because obviously  she can't personally be up on every area  of the world so she needed guidance from  each